[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Nobody had to read the bodies, so nobody did. We read all of them. The ticket holds three problems.
Each has its own ticket in [the split repo](https://github.com/pcierpiatka/s01e03-tenant-t3), with the count below.

<Callout kind="warn">
Three problems, three fixes. The one queued for #4471 fixes only the first.
</Callout>

## What the bodies show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

### `parser` · the shared parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first,
so 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the bug #4471 was opened for. It covers every channel and
every tenant, and intake tags most of it right.

### `hint` · booking-v3's form says MM/DD/YYYY and reads DD/MM

Three tenants run booking-v3. Its date field tells people to type month first, then the
form reads what they typed the tenant's way. The symptom is the same swap, but the fix is in
the form, not the parser: the kb's own `causeOf` already keeps it apart, and the old page
rendered it as "no ticket of its own". Now it has one. It only reaches us through the
`form` channel at those three tenants.

### `one-day` · bookings shift by one day, no month flip

A small group of bodies show no swap at all: an invite a day before the booking, "Tuesday
showed as Monday", a client in Lisbon and a client in Toronto holding the same meeting on
different days, every June booking one day off. The desk's notes already read them as a
one-day move but filed them under `parser` by tenant. They are not a date-order bug. They
look like a timezone or day-boundary bug, and a parser fix will not touch them.

## How we counted

- One id, one report: the export has duplicate rows, and `uniqueById` keeps one per id.
- A blind reader per tenant classified every body by what it says, seeing no tag and no note.
- Where the body and the kb's rule disagreed, an adjudicator placed the report and a second
  reader re-read it with a seeded sample of the rest.
- Bodies with no dates at all were placed by the kb's rule for their tenant and channel.
- The table above is computed from the id lists at render time. The same lists feed
  `tools/counts.py`, which checks that every report is placed exactly once.

## What to change

- Split #4471: keep it for `parser`, open the two others. Done in the split repo.
- Dedupe on cause, not subject, during incidents about dates: `sameCause` already exists.
- Fix the booking-v3 hint text first. It is a one-line change and stops new `hint` reports.

